On it, apart from chance wobbles. Almost half the patients (45%) drop out, but because drop-out has nothing to do with how sick they are, KM still gets it right: about 52% at 12 months (truth 52%) and 33% at 24 months (truth 35%). Lots of censoring makes the estimate less precise (wider CIs), but not biased.
Overestimate. With a similar amount of drop-out (41%), KM now says about 60% are alive at 12 months and 43% at 24 months: much better than the truth (52% and 35%). The sick patients who left would mostly have died, but KM assumes they’d have done as well as the healthier patients who stayed.
Underestimate. KM gives about 44% at 12 months and only 19% at 24 months. The patients who stayed are mostly the sicker ones, so the curve looks too pessimistic.
Yes, within each half the KM curve is close to its own true curve (allowing for chance), even though the overall curve was badly wrong. Within a half, dropping out tells you nothing more about a patient’s risk. So if you know why people drop out (here, how sick they are) and have measured it, you can allow for it, for example by analysing within groups or adjusting for it in a Cox model.






4.1.2 What you can do about it
The difficulty is that in real data, unlike in the app, you can’t see the true curve. Informative censoring can’t be detected from the survival data alone. What you can do is:

think about why patients were censored. Administrative censoring (the study ended) is usually harmless. Loss to follow-up deserves a closer look;
compare patients who were lost to follow-up with those who weren’t, on their characteristics at the start;
if drop-out depends on things you measured, allow for them (in a Cox model, or with more advanced methods such as inverse probability of censoring weighting);
report how many patients were lost to follow-up, so readers can judge.



