7.7 Reporting checklist
When writing up a PS-matched survival analysis, report:

the covariates in the PS model, and why they were chosen (ideally with a DAG);
the matching method, ratio, caliper, and with or without replacement;
the number of treated patients discarded, and how the matched population differs from the full one;
a balance table or Love plot, before and after matching (SMDs);
KM curves with risk tables for the matched cohort;
the HR with a robust CI, and a check of proportional hazards;
an absolute measure (survival difference or RMST);
a sensitivity analysis (e.g. E-value, different caliper, or IPTW: see Chapter 9);
how much data was missing, and how it was handled (Chapter 11).

The STROBE guidelines for observational studies are a useful framework.

7.7.1 Writing it up: an example
Here is how this chapter’s analysis might be written up. Adapt the bracketed details to your own study.

Methods. Propensity scores were estimated by logistic regression of treatment on age, sex, diabetes, BMI and smoking status. Each treated patient was matched to one untreated patient by nearest-neighbour matching without replacement on the logit of the propensity score, with a caliper of 0.2 standard deviations. Covariate balance was assessed with standardised mean differences (SMDs), with |SMD| &lt; 0.1 taken to indicate good balance. In the matched cohort, the effect of treatment on mortality was estimated with a Cox proportional hazards model, using robust standard errors clustered on matched pairs. Analyses used R with the survival, MatchIt and cobalt packages.
Results. Of 849 treated patients, 714 were matched to 714 untreated patients. 135 treated patients had no untreated patient within the caliper and were excluded. After matching, all SMDs were below 0.1 (the largest was 0.07, for age). There were 485 deaths in the matched cohort. Treatment was associated with a lower hazard of death (HR 0.68, 95% CI 0.57 to 0.81, p &lt; 0.001). Estimated 5-year survival was 69% (95% CI 65% to 73%) with treatment and 61% (95% CI 57% to 65%) without.


