The biggest dots are untreated patients in the top right (older, more severe: the kind of patient usually treated) and treated patients in the bottom left. They’re rare in their group, so each stands in for many similar patients. Both methods balance age and severity (|SMD| about 0.1 or less) and get close to the true effect of +2: matching gives +1.82, using 245 of the 354 treated patients, and IPTW gives +1.60, using everyone. The largest weight is about 15.
With strong confounding, the groups barely overlap. The largest weight is now 57, and the effective sample size of the treated group drops to 122 of 355. Weighting fails to balance severity (SMD 0.26), and the IPTW estimate is −0.50, far from +2. Matching does better (+1.34), but only by dropping more than half of the treated patients (167 of 355 kept). When overlap is poor, no method can estimate the effect for the patients who have no comparable counterparts.
